From 7f2c9a41d05e8b3762af1c0e94d5b3aa16c8f402 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 13:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the 24 MHz node is SYS_CLK24M, not the DCXO
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rename the `osc24M-clk` node to `sys24M-clk` and its `clock-output-names`
to "sys24M". The rate stays 24 MHz, deliberately, and no 26 MHz node is
added — that is the result of modelling the oscillator honestly, and the
reason is the whole point of this patch.

THE FILENAME PROMISES MORE THAN THE DIFF DELIVERS, ON PURPOSE

This patch was opened as "model the 26 MHz DCXO", from the 2026-07-21
finding that our DT was carrying `osc24M-clk { clock-frequency =
<24000000> }` on a board whose crystal is 26 MHz. Walking the actual
chain turned that into two separate findings:

  1. The node is misnamed, not mis-rated. It does not describe the
     crystal; it describes SYS_CLK24M, which really is 24 MHz on this
     board, and would still be 24 MHz on a 19.2 or 26 MHz one.
  2. The 26 MHz DCXO does belong in the tree — but as a clock provided by
     the block that owns the strap register (RTC-CCU, patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`), not as
     a hand-written DT literal.

So the honest fix is a rename here plus a phandle in `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`, and explicitly
*not* a second oscillator node. Changing the 24 MHz to 26 MHz — the
obvious reading of that finding, and the one upstream review of another
A733 DT for this board asked for — would have broken four correct
consumers to fix none.

THE CHAIN, WALKED

    X1 (crystal, 26.000 MHz)
      |
      +-- DCXO_ST0 / DCXO_ST1 strap pads tell the silicon which crystal
      |   is fitted; result mirrored read-only into XO_CTRL0[15:14]
      v
    DCXO --+--> PLL_REF  (N=96, P0=104)  --+
           |                               v
           +-----------------------------> mux --> SYS_CLK24M (24 MHz)
           +--> CPU_BACK_PLL, CPU_L_PLL, CPU_B_PLL, CPU_DSU_PLL
           +--> CPU_SLOW_CLK (the clusters' backup clock)
           +--> PLL_DIS0, PLL_DIS1, PLL_HDMI
           +--> UFS / COMBOPHY0+1 + HDMI PHY reference gates (XO_CTRL1)

    SYS_CLK24M --> AHB, APB0, APB1, APB_UART, TIMER, USB0/1/2, SMHC,
                   SPI, GMAC0, PIO+S_GPIO debounce, ... i.e. "hosc"

The DCXO branch scales with the crystal. The SYS_CLK24M branch does not:
PLL_REF exists precisely so that this net is 24 MHz whatever is soldered
on. Our single `osc24M` node sat on the second branch while its name
claimed the first.

WHERE EACH VALUE COMES FROM

Spec (A733 UM V0.92):

  * 7.1.4 Clock Sources gives both rates in one table:
    SYS_CLK24M is a fixed 24 MHz net, muxed from either DCXO or PLL_REF;
    DCXO is the board's external 26 MHz crystal.
    The same two-row table recurs per module.
  * 4.1.4.1: the real DCXO rate is read from XO_CTRL0_REG[15:14]; the
    REFPLL rate is fixed at 24M.
  * 9.1.5.4 DCXO Frequency: the two strap pads,
    weak pull-up, encoding 00 = 24M, 01 = 19.2M, 10 = 26M, 11 = 24M,
    readable in XO_CTRL0_REG[15:14]. The field table
    lists DCXO_ST as bits[15:14], access "R".
  * Table 4-2 carries a "Reference clock" column:
    26 MHz for PLL_REF and the four CPU PLLs, 24 MHz for PLL_DDR,
    PLL_PERI0/1, PLL_GPU0, PLL_VIDEO0/1/2, PLL_VE0/1, PLL_AUDIO0/1,
    PLL_NPU, PLL_DE. (Its last three rows — PLL_DIS0/DIS1/HDMI — read
    26 MHz too, but that part of the table did not convert cleanly to
    text; re-check it in the manual before relying on it.)
  * Table 4-3: REF_PLL, DCXO 26, N(INT) 96,
    VCO 2496 MHz, OUT 24 MHz. 2496 / 104 = 24 exactly.
  * 4.1.1: the CCU is fed by the external 26 MHz reference.

Silicon (register readback; `/sys/class/sunxi_dump`, 2026-07-22):

    XO_CTRL0 @0x07090160   the 6 GiB board = 0x983FB0F7   DCXO_ST[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz
                           a second 6 GiB board  = 0x183FB0F7   DCXO_ST[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz
                           the 8 GiB board = 0x983FB0F7

    Decode note: the register's own documented reset value is 0x983F_10F7.
    Our reads differ from it in exactly two read-only status fields —
    DCXO_ST (0b10) and SYSRST_STAT (de-asserted). Every writable field
    matches the documented default. So the 0b10 is the pads talking, not
    a leftover. A second 6 GiB board differs from the 6 GiB board only in bit31 CLK_REQ_ENB, an
    unrelated wake-up enable.

    PLL_REF @0x02002000    the 6 GiB board = 0xF8675F00
                           a second 6 GiB board  = 0xF8675F00
      -> PLL_N[15:8] = 0x5F -> N = 96, P0[22:16] = 0x67 -> 104
      -> 26 MHz x 96 / 104 = 24.000000 MHz.  With a 24 MHz input the same
         register would produce 22.15 MHz, which matches neither Table 4-3
         nor any peripheral rate this board runs.

    PLL_PERI0 @0x020020A0  the 6 GiB board = 0xFF126310 -> N = 100 -> 24 x 100 =
      2400 MHz = Table 4-3's PERI0_PLL row exactly (reference 24, not 26).

    APB_UART @0x02002538   the 6 GiB board = 0x00000000, a second 6 GiB board = 0x00000000
      -> src = 000 = SYS_CLK24M, M = 1.  This is UART0's parent, and the
         serial console has been legible at 115200 8N1 on every boot of
         this series. An 8250 divisor computed for 26 MHz on a 24 MHz
         input gives 124.8 kBd, +8.3 %, far outside an 8N1 frame's ~3 %
         budget. The working console is therefore a hardware measurement
         that this net is 24 MHz, independent of any register decode.

    APB1 @0x02002518       the 6 GiB board = 0x00000000 -> src = 000 = SYS_CLK24M.
      The pinctrl "apb" clock. Also live on index 0.

    CPU-PLL block, the 6 GiB board, for contrast (all four are on the DCXO branch):
      0x08870000 = 0xF8104A00  N=75, P0=2 -> 26 x 75 / 2 = 975 MHz.
        (The UM's CPU_BACK_PLL default note is no check on this: V0.92
        says 975 MHz "when DCXO is 26MHz", V1.00 says 900 MHz "when DCXO
        is 24MHz", PDF p. 305, for the same fields.)
      0x08871000 = 0xF8802E00  N=0x2E=46 -> 26 x 46 = 1196 MHz
      0x08872000 = 0xF8802000  N=0x20=32 -> 26 x 32 = 832, /2 = 416 MHz
      0x08873000 = 0xF8801E00  N=0x1E=30 -> 26 x 30 = 780 MHz
      Every one of these lands on a step of the measured OPP ladder.
      Multiplied by 24 none of them do.
      0x08873034 = 0x00000000  CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL = 00 = DCXO
        -- i.e. even the clusters' backup clock is the crystal.

No vendor BSP source, no sysfs and no clk_summary was used as a source
for any value above.

EVERY CONSUMER OF THIS NODE, AND WHETHER IT IS RIGHT

At this point in the series only the first two exist; the rest are listed
because the rename has to be safe for them too, and because two of them
need a follow-up this patch cannot reach.

  pio @0x02000000, "hosc"        CORRECT at 24 MHz. The pinctrl driver
      uses it only as the debounce clock, and PB_INT_DEB bit0
      GPIO_INT_CLK_SELECT selects RTC_32K (0) or SYS_CLK24M (1) (UM 18.14.6.16,
      A733 user manual) — SYS_CLK24M, not the DCXO. (Our node has no
      `input-debounce`, so pinctrl-sunxi.c never even reads the rate;
      sunxi_pinctrl_setup_debounce() returns early. The description is
      still what matters.)

  ccu @0x02002000, "hosc"        MOSTLY CORRECT, one wrong use, see the
      "what this does not fix" section. The AHB/APB0/APB1/APB_UART mux
      index 0 is SYS_CLK24M (UM 4.1.6 register descriptions: source 000 =
      SYS_CLK24M), and APB1 and APB_UART are sitting on it right now —
      24 MHz is required there, not merely allowed.

  usbphy @0x04100400, both       CORRECT at 24 MHz. The USB2 PHY runs off
      SYS_CLK24M; UM 9.1.5.5 lists the DCXO's two
      dedicated PHY reference outputs as UFS and COMBOPHY0/1 + HDMI, and
      USB2 is not among them. phy-sun4i-usb only enables the clock, so no
      rate is consumed either way.

  usb3 @0x06A00000               CORRECT and load-bearing:
      `assigned-clock-rates = <24000000>` on CLK_USB2_SUSPEND exists to
      force that mux off osc32k onto sys24M. Re-rating this node to
      26 MHz would have left the assignment asking for a rate its own
      parent can no longer produce.

  r_pio @0x07025000, "hosc"      CORRECT at 24 MHz — PL_INT_DEB bit0 is
      the same RTC_32K (0) / SYS_CLK24M (1) select (UM 18.14.7.15,
      A733 user manual).

  r_ccu @0x07010000, "hosc"      WRONG, and not fixable here. AHBS_CLK_REG
      bits[26:24] encode the source as 000 = DCXO, 001 = RTC_32K, 010 = RC16M,
      011 = PERIPLL_DIV (200 MHz), 100 = PERI0_300M (UM 4.2.5.1)
      — index 0 on the R-CCU is the crystal itself, 26 MHz,
      not SYS_CLK24M. The error is latent today: the 6 GiB board reads AHBS
      0x03000000, APBS0 0x03000001, APBS1 0x04000000, i.e. src 011/011/100,
      so index 0 is not selected on any of the three. The r_ccu node is
      created by patch `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`, which applies after this one, so correcting
      it belongs to a patch in that range and is recorded here rather than
      silently left.

WHY RENAME AND NOT RE-RATE, SPLIT, OR ADD A NODE

  * Re-rate to 26 MHz: breaks pio, ccu (two live mux inputs), usbphy,
    usb3 and r_pio to fix nothing — the DCXO consumers are the CPU PLLs,
    which do not reference this node at all. It would also have put a
    26 MHz number under a name that four subsystems read as "the 24 MHz
    reference".

  * Split into two fixed-clocks (24 MHz + 26 MHz in the DT): rejected
    because it creates a second, weaker source for a net that patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`
    already derives from the silicon. Two providers of one physical clock
    can disagree, and the DT literal is the one that would be wrong on a
    board strapped differently. It would also register a second clock
    named "dcxo" in the framework alongside `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`'s.

  * Leave alone: the name is the bug. It is what made this take a year of
    patches to notice, and it is the point upstream review of an A733 SoC
    dtsi for this board has already raised (below).

  * Rename: the label `osc24M` is kept as an alias on the same node so
    the rest of the series keeps resolving; new nodes use `&sys24M`.
    Labels do not exist in the dtb, so the artefact is unambiguous, and
    the two-label form costs nothing. This is the one place where "one
    file only" and "no half-migration" pull against each other; keeping
    the alias means no other patch in the series has to be touched.

HOW THIS COMPOSES WITH `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` AND `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`

Patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` adds CLK_RTC_DCXO (= 2) to the RTC-CCU, decoding the strap out
of XO_CTRL0[15:14] on the running silicon. Patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`'s cpupll_ccu node
takes `clocks = <&rtc_ccu CLK_RTC_DCXO>`. That is the right place for it
and this patch deliberately does not compete with it: the DT here
references neither CLK_RTC_DCXO nor a 26 MHz literal.

The main CCU could not use CLK_RTC_DCXO even if we wanted to, and the
reason is structural rather than a preference: rtc_ccu takes `r-ahb` from
r_ccu, and r_ccu takes pll-periph-200m/300m from ccu. Pointing ccu's
"hosc" at rtc_ccu closes a three-node loop in the DT clock graph. Whether
Linux would survive it via orphan parents is beside the point — it is not
a topology to hand to a reviewer. If the CCU ever needs a real DCXO input
(see below), it needs a board-level fixed-clock, and that is a decision to
make in the patch that adds the consumer, not here.

UPSTREAM

Upstream review of an A733 SoC dtsi for the Cubie A7S (linux-sunxi,
June 2026, Link below) noted that the A7S and A7A schematics show a
26 MHz crystal, that the board DT needs a way to set the crystal rate,
and that the node and clock name are a bit misleading. This patch takes up the
naming point. For the rate it goes another way on this SoC: this node is
SYS_CLK24M, not the crystal, so its rate stays 24 MHz, and the crystal's
26 MHz reaches Linux through the register that reports it (CLK_RTC_DCXO).
If this tree is ever submitted, this patch documents that choice, and it
keeps the upstream path open: after it the tree names two nets that the
UM names.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX — a live bug this investigation surfaced

`pll-ref` in ccu-sun60iw2.c (patch `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`) is registered with parent
`{ .fw_name = "hosc" }`, i.e. this node. Physically PLL_REF's input is the
DCXO, not SYS_CLK24M. With the DT saying 24 MHz the driver computes
24 x 96 / 104 = 22.15 MHz instead of 24.000 MHz, and every clock below it
— pll-peri0 and all its taps, hence ahb/apb when they are on peri0, the
SMHC card clocks and the rate stmmac reads for its MDC divider — is
modelled 7.7 % low. This is arithmetic on the driver's own parent
declaration and the register value, not a measurement, and it has never
been observed because nothing on this board fails at a 7.7 % clock error.

It is not fixed here because it cannot be: the CCU driver uses one "hosc"
input for two different nets, so separating them is a driver change (give
the CCU a "dcxo" input and derive an internal sys24m from pll-ref, or
parent pll-ref to a board fixed-clock). Fixing the DT alone would move the
error from pll-ref onto the four mux slots instead of removing it. Left
as a follow-up for the CCU driver rather than folded in.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED

Verified: applies clean on the full series, and also on the
fully-patched tree (offset 5). `dtc` builds the DTS with zero
warnings. The outcome was inspected, not the exit code: decompiling the
resulting dtb and diffing it against the dtb built without this patch
gives exactly two changed lines — the node name `osc24M-clk` ->
`sys24M-clk` and `clock-output-names` "osc24M" -> "sys24M". The node keeps
phandle 0x17 and all five `clocks` properties that referenced it
(pinctrl@2000000, clock-controller@2002000, phy@4100400,
clock-controller@7010000, pinctrl@7025000) still resolve to 0x17. Patch
`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` applies on top unchanged.

Not verified: no kernel was built and this has not been booted. The
register reads above are silicon; everything downstream of them is spec
plus arithmetic.

PENDING — one falsifiable observation on hardware

One boot of this series on the 6 GiB board or the 8 GiB board with the new dtb: the serial
console must stay legible at 115200 8N1 from the first kernel message to
the shell, in the same session in which `devmem 0x02002538` reads
0x00000000 (APB_UART on src 000 = SYS_CLK24M). That pair is the whole
claim: it says the node this patch renamed still describes a 24 MHz net,
which is what makes the rename correct and the obvious re-rate wrong. Had
this patch set the node to 26 MHz, the console would be garbage from
earlycon onward — so the observation cannot pass by accident.

